{"author":{"id":"a66e0db0201c515c5090d5e52c036411dda9ba2f138110a926616a18a8ea0dd6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790979368,"nonce":"Cce/VT9V2qdPivWhDP3WpTj7vAA=","title":"a second getCompilationInfo on a shader hangs on the web while the device is idle","message":"Chrome answers GPUShaderModule.getCompilationInfo() the first time it is asked for a module. A second call on the same module, valid or not, stays pending until the device has other work: a `queue.submit` releases it, and with nothing submitted it does not settle.\n\nFound 2026-10-02 in HeadlessChrome 154 on macOS through Ash's page, and reproduced in plain JavaScript in the same page:\n\n    const m = device.createShaderModule({ code: \"@compute @workgroup_size(1) fn main() {}\" });\n    await m.getCompilationInfo();            // resolves\n    m.getCompilationInfo();                  // pending after 5 s\n    const again = m.getCompilationInfo();\n    device.queue.submit([]);\n    await again;                             // resolves\n\nIn xgpu this hung a program at `GpuShader.messages()` after it had awaited `getCompilationInfo()` for the same shader: `shader_messages` waits for the answer while nothing else reaches the device. A program awaiting `getCompilationInfo()` twice with no GPU work between would hang the same way.\n\nA shader's messages never change, so web.rs asks once per shader and keeps them: `shader_messages` and a hand-written `shader_compilation_info` (in place of x-idl's generated forward) both read that.","files":null}]}